Robert Cailliau was born in Tongeren, Belgium, in 1947. He studied engineering at Ghent University and the University of Michigan, and he joined CERN, the European physics laboratory near Geneva, in 1974.

## Working with Tim Berners-Lee

In 1987 Cailliau proposed his own hypertext system for CERN. When Tim Berners-Lee built the World Wide Web in late 1990, the two co-wrote a proposal to fund the project. Cailliau became the web's biggest supporter inside CERN. He ran student projects to build browsers for different computers, and with Nicola Pellow he helped create MacWWW, the first browser for the Mac.

## Spreading the web

- **1993:** Worked with CERN's legal team on the document that released the web's technology into the public domain on 30 April 1993.
- **1994:** Organized the first International World Wide Web Conference at CERN, which brought together 380 web pioneers.
- **1995:** Started the "Web for Schools" project with the European Commission, and helped move web development from CERN to the W3C.
- **1995:** Shared the ACM Software System Award with Berners-Lee.
- **2012:** Inducted into the Internet Hall of Fame.

He also designed the original multicolored WWW logo and co-wrote *How the Web Was Born*, the first book-length history of the web.

